L599: Effects of vitamin D 3  supplementation on training‐associated changes in muscle fibre characteristics and transcriptomics
L600: Participants in both vitamin D 3  and placebo arms showed marked changes in muscle fibre characteristics over the course of the training intervention. These included decreased type IIX muscle fibre proportions from 10% to 7% ( Figure 
L601:  9 ), increased type IIA proportions from 26% to 29% ( Figure 
L602:  9 ), increased type IIA/IIX hybrid fibres abundances from 2.6% to 3.2% (Supporting Information,  Table 
L603:  S2 ), and 25–48% increases in myonuclei number per muscle fibre ( Figure 
L604:  9 ). Changes in IIX and IIA proportions were verified using qPCR, showing decreased levels of type IIX mRNA abundance and increased levels of type IIA ( Figure 
L605:  9 ), calculated using the gene family‐profiling approach. 
L606:  76 
L607:   These analyses also revealed increased proportions of type I mRNA after the training intervention ( Figure 
L608:  9 ), potentially caused by increased type I protein turnover. The observed changes in muscle fibre‐type characteristics corroborate well with previous studies in older adults, 
